WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.780
Smell and taste both detect chemicals.

2
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.460
Why do they read them so differently?

3
00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:16.880
In a mouse, most mature olfactory neurons express one receptor gene at high

4
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.620
abundance, chosen from roughly a thousand.

5
00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.580
That is a statement about genes, not about odorants.

6
00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:27.120
One receptor can still bind many odorants.

7
00:00:27.883 --> 00:00:30.243
Where do their axons go?

8
00:00:31.183 --> 00:00:36.783
In mice, axons from neurons that share one receptor start scattered across the

9
00:00:36.783 --> 00:00:38.143
olfactory epithelium.

10
00:00:38.943 --> 00:00:43.163
They converge on two fixed glomeruli in each olfactory bulb.

11
00:00:43.723 --> 00:00:47.523
So each glomerulus collects mainly from one receptor type.

12
00:00:48.183 --> 00:00:51.983
The glomerular layer is largely a map of receptor identity.

13
00:00:52.833 --> 00:01:00.593
Here is an invented example with four receptors, A to D. Odorant 2 activates B and

14
00:01:00.593 --> 00:01:08.893
C. Odorant 3 activates A, B and C. Imaging of mouse olfactory neurons found the same

15
00:01:08.893 --> 00:01:12.813
two things. One receptor recognized several odorants.

16
00:01:13.453 --> 00:01:16.133
One odorant was recognized by several receptors.

17
00:01:17.117 --> 00:01:26.137
Now remove receptor A. Odorant 2 still gives B and C. Odorant 3 now gives B and C as

18
00:01:26.137 --> 00:01:29.997
well. The model predicts they can no longer be told apart.

19
00:01:30.997 --> 00:01:38.157
Odorant 1 activates A and B. At tenfold concentration it also recruits receptor C, a

20
00:01:38.157 --> 00:01:39.337
lower-affinity receptor.

21
00:01:40.077 --> 00:01:42.317
Its pattern now matches odorant 3.

22
00:01:42.997 --> 00:01:45.837
The model predicts it could be mistaken for odorant 3.

23
00:01:45.837 --> 00:01:49.357
This is a toy model, not a count of smells.

24
00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:52.247
Taste works differently.

25
00:01:53.067 --> 00:01:58.847
In mouse taste buds, sweet, umami and bitter cells carry different receptors.

26
00:01:59.907 --> 00:02:05.647
After the receptor, they share the same steps, including the enzyme PLCβ2.

27
00:02:06.547 --> 00:02:10.347
If the machinery is shared, what keeps sweet and bitter apart?

28
00:02:11.627 --> 00:02:14.127
Zhang and colleagues removed PLCβ2.

29
00:02:14.707 --> 00:02:18.767
The tested sweet, amino acid and bitter responses were lost.

30
00:02:18.767 --> 00:02:21.767
Sour and salty responses were spared.

31
00:02:22.667 --> 00:02:28.887
Then they restored PLCβ2 only in cells that express bitter receptors.

32
00:02:29.727 --> 00:02:30.967
Bitter responses returned.

33
00:02:31.667 --> 00:02:34.307
Sweet and amino acid responses did not.

34
00:02:35.227 --> 00:02:40.087
The shared cascade carried whichever quality its cell was wired to report.

35
00:02:40.807 --> 00:02:43.287
These are mouse experiments on the periphery.

36
00:02:44.087 --> 00:02:45.527
Smell reads a pattern.

37
00:02:46.487 --> 00:02:48.087
Taste reads which cell fires.
